WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.840
Matt Reeves' "The Batman" introduces a brand&nbsp;
new version of the Caped Crusader to audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:11.600
fleshing out Bruce Wayne in ways fans haven't seen&nbsp;
in live-action before. First, the film forgoes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.800
typical origin story of that fateful night when&nbsp;
Thomas and Martha Wayne entered Crime Alley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.200
Instead, it opts to drop on Batman in the&nbsp;
second year of his war on crime in Gotham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.520
It's a clever way of giving audiences a young,&nbsp;
fresh-faced take on the character without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.560
retreading too much familiar ground. It seems this&nbsp;
narrative decision is working with audiences and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.600
critics alike because "The Batman" has earned&nbsp;
itself a healthy rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.560
"The Batman" story also introduces a vicious new&nbsp;
version of the Riddler, who is much more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.920
homegrown terrorist than Jim Carrey's mischievous&nbsp;
trickster in "Batman Forever." This time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.800
the villain's motivation centers around exposing&nbsp;
and punishing the corrupt members of Gotham's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.480
political and legislative infrastructure, starting&nbsp;
with Mayor Don Mitchell, Jr.. Riddler murders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.280
Mitchell in the film's opening, and it's this&nbsp;
brutal act that kicks off a sadistic killing spree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.560
that threatens to unleash utter chaos on Gotham.&nbsp;
With every kill, the Riddler ropes Batman deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.200
into a game of cat-and-mouse that gets way more&nbsp;
personal than Bruce Wayne could have imagined.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.880
The Riddler puts the Wayne family in his&nbsp;
crosshairs because he believes Thomas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.360
Martha Wayne had as much responsibility&nbsp;
for Gotham's downfall as Mayor Mitchell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.000
the mobster Carmine Falcone, and other&nbsp;
high-profile Gotham figures with shady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.880
backgrounds. The masked killer goes deep into&nbsp;
the truth about the Waynes in a tell-all video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.640
revealing many dark secrets about Bruce's parents&nbsp;
— including their connection to the Arkham family.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:39.040
So, how are the Waynes tied to the Arkhams?&nbsp;
In "The Batman," we learn Thomas Wayne married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.560
Martha Arkham. Yes, that's right, Martha is part&nbsp;
of the Arkham family. It's a surprise for fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.200
to be sure. Previous live-action "Batman" movies&nbsp;
have never looked at the history of the Waynes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.440
(including those who married into the family)&nbsp;
in great detail. There's the throwaway line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.400
in Christopher Nolan's trilogy about one of Bruce&nbsp;
Wayne's ancestors building an underground railroad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.600
to help slaves escape, which helps explain the&nbsp;
existence of the caves underneath Wayne Manor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.760
But, aside from that, we don't know much&nbsp;
about the Wayne family tree. Until now.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:12.160
In "The Batman," the Dark Knight's investigation&nbsp;
leads him to the abandoned orphanage that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.720
originally supported by The Wayne Foundation&nbsp;
before the untimely deaths of Thomas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.360
Martha. He's led to the run-down location&nbsp;
after it is mentioned in a shocking video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.200
released by the Riddler that shines a&nbsp;
light on the Waynes in great detail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.760
The villain explains that Martha was born&nbsp;
into the Arkham family. In her youth, Martha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.880
dealt with mental illness and, per Riddler, had&nbsp;
several stays in the Arkham Asylum to treat it.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.240
The Riddler's video on the Waynes&nbsp;
also reveals that Edward Elliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.440
a journalist employed by Sal Maroni, found out&nbsp;
about Martha's past and threatened to expose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.360
news to derail Thomas' mayoral campaign. Bruce's&nbsp;
father leans on Carmine Falcone for support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.600
asking the gangster to intimidate the&nbsp;
journalist so the story can stay under wraps.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.040
This is why Riddler targets the Wayne legacy&nbsp;
— it looks like Thomas had Elliot killed.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.280
Anyone even remotely familiar with the Batman&nbsp;
mythos will probably recognize the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.920
"Arkham" thanks to the notoriety of Arkham&nbsp;
Asylum. Typically, it's Arkham Asylum where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.320
the Caped Crusader sends his opponents after&nbsp;
beating them black and blue in the streets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.320
Tying Batman directly to Arkham through his&nbsp;
mother's bloodline is an interesting way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.280
making this new universe feel interconnected,&nbsp;
rather than playing with random chess pieces.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.800
There's also some extensive history&nbsp;
to the asylum in the comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.880
The location was first introduced in&nbsp;
1974's "Batman" #258 and was known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:35.680
Elizabeth Arkham Asylum for the Criminally&nbsp;
Insane. Grant Morrison's "Arkham Asylum:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.960
A Serious House on Serious Earth" establishes&nbsp;
that the iconic location was originally founded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:46.960
by Amadeus Arkham in the early 1900s after&nbsp;
his mother died by suicide. He transformed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.760
family home into an institution before his wife&nbsp;
and daughter were killed by his first patient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.520
Martin Hawkins. This eventually drove Amadeus&nbsp;
mad, and he was locked up in his own asylum.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.600
But when it comes to Bruce's mother, her&nbsp;
maiden name is Martha Kane in the comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.840
It wasn't until Geoff Johns' "Batman: Earth&nbsp;
One" that DC introduced her Arkham heritage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.720
She also suffers from mental illness in the&nbsp;
2012 series, and there's also the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:18.320
Arkham Manor is cursed, supposedly dooming any&nbsp;
future generation to eventual mental illness.

